"""Authored mathematical explanations, kept separate from machine exports."""
from pathlib import Path
import json
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
nodes=json.loads((ROOT/'content/base-notes.json').read_text())
def note(key,title,body,refs='',children=(),kind='explanation'):
    nodes[key]={'title':title,'body':body,'sources':refs.split(),'children':list(children),'kind':kind}

# Replace the prototype's stopping points with routes into the full proof explorer.
note('finite-count','Finite counting, down to its formal proof','Open the cardinality theorem below. Its dependencies give the precise finite-set bijections and counting identities used. Each dependency can in turn be opened, including library proofs.','Core:card_guardPairs')
note('runtime-boundary','Correctness and the running-time theorem','The parsing theorem specifies exactly which bit strings encode an input. The acceptance theorem connects the fixed RAM program to that same language. The polynomial theorem bounds executions of that program on every bit string, so it includes rejection paths. The older cost-monad theorem remains in the source as an earlier result; MMain supplies the later machine theorem.','Parsing:decodeInput_eq_some_iff Parsing:decideBits_iff_StrictBits MMain:program_accepts_iff MMain:program_polynomial')
note('limit-boundary','The analytic proof is included','The finite counting bounds are cast into real inequalities. Logarithms turn the fixed multiplicative constants into additive constants, which vanish after division by log n. Open the theorem and follow its analytic dependencies; they are part of the exported proof graph.','GeneralAsymptotics:log_ratio_tendsto\'')
note('canonical','Why processing children first identifies equal syntax','A compound term is determined by its symbol and its ordered child terms. Once earlier children have canonical identifiers, equality of these records is equivalent to equality of the terms they represent. The executable implementation keeps this sharing, so it never needs to unfold a shared graph into a large tree.','EncodedSyntax:Instance.canonIds MCanon:canonIdsM_spec')
note('split-network','From vertex cuts to a finite flow computation','Splitting a vertex puts capacity one on its entrance-to-exit edge. A path using that term must cross this edge. The other connections have sufficiently large capacity, so a minimum cut can be chosen among the unit edges. The formal implementation uses lists of unit arcs, with repeated arcs representing capacity. The correspondence theorem proves that its computed flow value is exactly the semantic term-cut number.','FlowCut:rhoFlow_eq TermMenger:mengerInput')
note('class-c','Exactly which hypotheses define the input class','The two variables are distinct, both occur among the outputs, and their disequality occurs among the tests. Extra outputs and variables are allowed. These hypotheses are needed to include the retained pair in every cut and to obtain the sharper cardinality bound.','GeneralAsymptotics:InClassC TermGraph:retained_pair_mem_cut')
note('structural','Finite constructors and structural recursion','Term has a variable constructor and an application constructor with an ordered family of children. Evaluation follows these constructors. Open the inductive declaration to inspect its constructors and elimination rules; open evaluation to inspect its defining equations. Recursion is justified by Lean’s termination machinery.','Threshold:Term Threshold:eval')
note('equality-rule','Equality reflexivity and substitution','Equality has a reflexivity constructor. Its eliminator transports a statement along an equality. In the proof explorer, a reference to Eq.refl or Eq.rec opens the exact declaration and, for a recursor, its reduction rule. This is a logical endpoint, rather than an unproved mathematical lemma.','Core:avoidance_iff_identity',kind='foundation')
note('classical-logic','Classical reasoning is explicit','The source proof reasons by contradiction. Its checked proof term records the resulting dependencies. Classical.choice and propositional extensionality are axioms of the chosen foundations; they have statements to inspect but no proof bodies in this development.','Core:avoidance_iff_identity',kind='foundation')

note('s1-1','Expressions before interpretations','A term is syntax. Evaluation requires both an interpretation of every symbol and an assignment of every variable. The same interpretation is passed recursively into every child. This enforces the shared use of a symbol throughout the entire instance.',children=['syntax','tables','evaluation'])
note('s1-2','Dispersion is a maximum of image sizes','First filter assignments by all the tests. Then apply the output tuple and remove repeated outputs. Finally maximise the size of this image over all interpretations on a fixed finite alphabet. The maximum exists because there are finitely many such interpretations.',children=['valid','image','maximum','attainment'])
note('s1-3','One input class for every degree','The parameter k changes the threshold, not the definition of an instance. The class only insists on a retained pair of distinct source variables and its disequality. It does not insist on k variables or k outputs.','GeneralAsymptotics:InClassC',children=['class-c'])
note('s1-4','The two thresholds differ by one output','Choose the first coordinate in n ways, the second in n−1 ways, and each remaining coordinate in n ways. This counts bₖ(n). Because dispersion is an integer, exceeding bₖ(n) is equivalent to reaching bₖ(n)+1. The mathematical predicates and the two complexity directions are separate formal declarations.','SharpCut:threshold_eq SharpCut:guard_count_eq_threshold DegreeThreshold:LowerT DegreeThreshold:StrictT',children=['headline-hard','headline-easy'])
note('s1-5','The cut includes zero-length paths','A variable which is an output supplies a path with no edges. A cut must meet it at that variable. In particular x and y belong to every cut. Repeated outputs identify the same graph vertex.','TermGraph:two_le_cutSize',children=['cut-definition','zero-paths','support'])
note('s1-6','The headline theorem, claim by claim','This statement combines a semantic criterion, an algorithm with a machine running-time bound, and a reduction whose undecidability conclusion uses an external theorem. The links below keep those obligations separate so that the assumptions cannot be hidden inside a single “verified” badge.',children=['headline-hard','headline-easy','headline-witness','headline-bound'])
note('headline-hard','Why the non-strict problem is undecidable','For each fixed k≥2, the compiler pads a two-output group instance to k retained variables. Its non-strict answer agrees exactly with finite-quotient separation. The noncomputability theorem explicitly takes the finite-group noncomputability hypothesis as an argument.','DegreeCompiler:Lower_compileDegree_iff_quotient DegreeCompiler:not_computablePred_Lower_degree',children=['external-group','padding-step'])
note('headline-easy','Why the strict problem is polynomial time','The semantic theorem identifies the answer with distinct test sides and a cut of size at least k+1. The encoding and max-flow theorems connect this condition to decideBits. The RAM theorem proves a uniform polynomial bound for a fixed program implementing that decision.','TermMenger:strict_degree_iff\' FlowCut:strictDecideP_iff Parsing:decideBits_iff_StrictBits MMain:program_polynomial MMain:program_accepts_iff',children=['strict-arithmetic','machine-model'])
note('headline-witness','Why the alphabet size s^(k+1) suffices','Routing gives floor(n/s)^(k+1) outputs. Substituting n=s^(k+1) makes this n^k, which is at least n^k−n^(k−1)+1 because n≥2. This is a sufficient alphabet size; it is not a claim that all operation tables can be printed quickly.','DegreeThreshold:witness_arithmetic TermMenger:strict_witness\' WitnessSize:witness_size_uniform')
note('headline-bound','Why no computable bound on witness size can exist','A computable upper bound would turn the unbounded search for an alphabet into a finite search. At each size, all interpretations and assignments form finite sets. Searching them would decide the non-strict problem, contradicting the reduction. No efficiency assumption is needed for that contradiction.','GeneralSemidecision:no_computable_witness_bound_degree',children=['finite-search','external-group'])
note('s1-7','Value, bit length and output tables','For fixed k, s^(k+1) is a polynomial in s. For variable k the value may be large, but positivity implies k+1≤ρ≤s. The binary length of s^(k+1) is bounded by (k+1) times the binary length of s. This bounds the integer describing the alphabet, not tables on that alphabet.','WitnessSize:size_pow_le WitnessSize:witness_size_uniform',children=['headline-witness'])
note('s1-8','Saturation turns tests into laws','If every distinct pair is admitted, fixing a and choosing b=F(a) rules out F(a)≠a. Applying this separately to each law term forces all inverse and relator identities. Padding then preserves exactly whether saturation is possible.',children=['s4-2','s4-5','s5-5','padding-step','external-group'])
note('s1-9','Routing and the sharper upper bound','The labels are part of alphabet elements. They tell a shared operation which subterm it is evaluating and simultaneously separate syntactically distinct sides of tests. The upper bound counts values on a cut, with x≠y removing one diagonal factor. The historical attribution is bibliographic context; the linked theorems are this development’s formal results.',children=['s3-6','s3-7','sharp','routing-credit'])
note('s1-10','Approximation does not decide saturation','A shift interpretation makes every nonempty input word move every point when n exceeds its written length. Each law can then exclude at most one extra second coordinate in each row. Losing at most Mn pairs gives density approaching one, even if losing zero pairs is impossible.','Density:compiledMax_bounds Density:compiledRatio_tendsto Density:density_guarantee',children=['shift-step','s7-7'])
note('s1-11','Three exact examples','Distinct retained variables make all allowed assignments distinct outputs. Repeating the last coordinate loses that free coordinate without changing the number of output positions. The final test x₁≠x₁ is deliberately impossible: it removes every assignment and makes the dispersion zero, so both questions have answer “no”. These constructions show that the decision problems are nonvacuous.','GeneralExamples:identity_example GeneralExamples:repeat_example GeneralExamples:identical_test_not_lower GeneralSemantics:identical_test_dispersionTuple_zero')
note('s1-12','An easy subset of a hard language','A strict positive instance is non-strict positive because bₖ(n)+1≥bₖ(n). Inclusion alone imposes no relation in the reverse direction between the complexities of recognising the sets. The arithmetic separation bₖ(n)+1<bₖ₊₁(n) gives the alternating sequence.','DegreeThreshold:lowerT_of_strictT DegreeThreshold:threshold_interleave')
note('s1-13','Scope of the correction','This is an editorial withdrawal and a description of the relationship to the earlier papers. It is not a Lean theorem. The proofs here concern disequality tests; none of the linked formal statements asserts undecidability of the problem without tests.',kind='scope')

note('external-group','The external finite-group theorem','The imported mathematical input says that some fixed finite presentation has a noncomputable finite-quotient separation predicate. This project proves implications from that hypothesis; it does not prove the Slobodskoi–Bridson–Wilton theorem. In Lean, the hypothesis appears explicitly as ¬ ComputablePred (HasFiniteQuotient rels), rather than as a new hidden axiom. The paper cites Bridson–Wilton, Theorem 2.1.','PresentationBridge:HasFiniteQuotient GeneralInterface:all_degree_headline',kind='external input')
note('s5-1','Precisely what comes from group theory','HasFiniteQuotient quantifies over a finite group and a surjective homomorphism, with a nonidentity value for the word. The existence of a fixed presentation with a noncomputable predicate is external. Finite enumeration and the reduction are proved in this project.','PresentationBridge:HasFiniteQuotient',children=['external-group','finite-search'])
note('s5-2','The compiler keeps the written order of letters','A signed letter chooses one of two independent unary symbols. A list of letters becomes nested application, with the last letter applied first. Two inverse laws per generator and one law per relator are retained as a list, including repetitions and empty relators.','CompilerBridge:wordTerm Core:signedTables EncodedCompiler:laws',children=['word-order'])
note('word-order','Why the permutation product matches nesting','Group words multiply from left to right as written; acting on a point applies the rightmost permutation first. The evaluation bridge proves that nested unary evaluation equals the action of that product. Inverse cancellation is only available after the tables satisfy the inverse laws.','Core:evalWord_perm Core:evalWord_mul_left ReducedWords:groupWord_reduce')
note('s5-3','The actual encoded tests','The compiler emits the guard x≠y, a disequality L(x)≠y for every law, and the word test τw(x)≠x. The output pair itself is unchanged. The semantic bridge proves that the allowed image of these terms is exactly the pair set used in the counting argument.','CompilerBridge:compilerTests CompilerBridge:valid_compiler_iff CompilerBridge:compiler_image_eq EncodedCompiler:compileWord_testTerms')
note('s5-4','The equivalence has two constructive directions','Saturation supplies permutations satisfying the relators, hence a separating finite quotient. A separating quotient supplies a regular action whose word has no fixed point, hence saturation. The compiler builds a finite syntax description for these same tests.','CompilerBridge:hard_threshold_iff_quotient PresentationBridge:finite_cardinality_iff_quotient',children=['s5-5','s5-6','compiler-size'])
note('s5-5','From saturation to a separating quotient','First extract the everywhere-valid laws. Two-sided inverses turn pᵢ into permutations. Relators equal to the identity allow the universal property of the presented group to produce a homomorphism into the finite permutation group. The word moves every point, so its permutation is not the identity. Taking the image subgroup makes the homomorphism surjective.','Core:compiledPerfect_to_group PresentationBridge:finite_model_iff_presentation_hom PresentationBridge:finite_hom_iff_quotient',children=['s4-5','presentation-property'])
note('presentation-property','Where the presentation relations are used','The group is constructed as a quotient of a free group by the normal closure of the relators. A map on generators extends through that quotient exactly when it sends each relator to the identity. Open the bridge theorem, then its PresentedGroup and free-group dependencies, to inspect that construction.','PresentationBridge:WordPresentation PresentationBridge:lift_freeWord PresentationBridge:finite_model_iff_presentation_hom')
note('s5-6','From a finite quotient to saturation','Left multiplication by a nonidentity group element has no fixed point: qb=b implies q=1 by cancellation. The regular action satisfies all presentation laws. Thus all distinct pairs satisfy all tests. A group with a nonidentity element has at least two elements.','Core:group_to_compiledPerfect Core:evalWord_mul_left PresentationBridge:finite_model_iff_quotient',children=['compiler-size'])
note('compiler-size','Why the written compiler is small','Each letter contributes one unary application node. The inverse-law prefix has fixed length for a fixed presentation, and the relators are copied once. The exact node-length formulas expose this linear size calculation. The executable compiler is also proved computable; the source-level linear construction argument explains the paper’s polynomial bound.','EncodedCompiler:wordNodes_length EncodedCompiler:compileWord_nodes_length\' EncodedCompiler:compileWord_tests_length DegreeCompiler:computable_compileDegree')
note('s5-7','Fixing the presentation fixes the signature and test count','The only varying term represents the input word. The number of tests is 2d+q+2, independent of that word’s length. The reduction transfers the external noncomputability result to the encoded instance language. Positive instances can still be enumerated.','EncodedCompiler:compileWord_tests_length DegreeCompiler:not_computablePred_Lower_degree GeneralSemidecision:rePred_Lower_degree GeneralSemidecision:not_rePred_not_Lower_degree',children=['external-group','finite-search'])
note('s5-8','Why the positive cases are enumerable but the negative cases are not','At each alphabet size there are finitely many tables and assignments, so the fixed-size threshold can be computed. Searching sizes finds every positive case. If the complement were enumerable too, dovetailing the two searches would decide the language.','GeneralSemidecision:rePred_Lower_degree GeneralSemidecision:not_rePred_not_Lower_degree',children=['finite-search'])
note('finite-search','The finite search is an actual formal construction','Finite tables represent all interpretations, and a finite assignment list represents every input. The algorithm collects distinct outputs passing the tests. Its correctness and computability connect this enumeration to the semantic maximum. It can be very slow; only finiteness and computability are required here.','GeneralSemidecision:searchComputable GeneralSemidecision:boundedAnyG_iff GeneralSemidecision:computable_boundedAnyG')
note('s5-9','Free reduction belongs to group evaluation','The reduction algorithm removes adjacent opposite signed letters. It preserves the value of a word in every group, and hence finite-quotient separation. It does not preserve evaluation under arbitrary unrelated unary functions. The latter distinction is essential for the shift construction.','ReducedWords:groupWord_reduce ReducedWords:hasFiniteQuotient_reduce ReducedWords:computable_reduce ReducedWords:not_computable_iff_reduced',children=['s7-7'])
note('s5-10','Empty words and the limit of the complexity claim','The empty word evaluates to x by definition, so its last test is impossible. Removing that one decidable negative case cannot make an undecidable language decidable: a purported decision procedure for the remainder could handle the empty case separately. Enumerability plus undecidability does not establish completeness.','CompilerBridge:wordTerm GeneralSemantics:identical_test_dispersionTuple_zero',children=['s5-9'],kind='explanation')

note('padding-step','Why padding gives an exact product','The new variables occur in no old output or test. For each old allowed output, all n^(k−2) choices of new coordinates occur, independently. Conversely, projecting any new output onto its first two coordinates gives an old allowed output. This proves equality of sets, then cardinalities. The multiplier is independent of the interpretation, so it commutes with taking the maximum.','Padding:padded_filteredImage Padding:padded_filteredImage_card Padding:finset_sup_mul_const Padding:padding_dispersion')
note('s6-1','Padding transfers saturation to every fixed degree','The exact product identity multiplies the pair bound by n^(k−2). This factor is positive for n≥2, so reaching the new upper bound is equivalent to reaching the old one. All k variables are outputs, forcing cut size k.','DegreeCompiler:compileDegree_dispersion DegreeCompiler:compileDegree_cutSize DegreeCompiler:Lower_compileDegree_iff_quotient',children=['padding-step','external-group'])
note('strict-arithmetic','Both sides of the strict criterion','If the cut is at most k, monotonicity of bρ in ρ and the sharp cut bound prevent any strict witness. If the cut is at least k+1, routing at n=s^(k+1) gives at least n^k outputs. Since n^(k−1)≥1, this reaches bₖ(n)+1. An identical-side test is a separate immediate rejection case.','SharpCut:threshold_mono DegreeThreshold:dispersionTuple_le_threshold_of_cut_le DegreeThreshold:witness_arithmetic TermMenger:strict_degree_iff\'')
note('s6-2','The strict criterion and the sufficient size','The negative direction uses the sharper bound, not just D≤n^ρ. The positive direction substitutes an explicit size into routing. The degree comparison can be performed on binary integers; the decision procedure never needs to construct the alphabet or the operation tables.',children=['strict-arithmetic','headline-witness','machine-model'])
note('s6-3','A bounded witness search would decide the hard problem','Compute the encoded length, compute its alleged bound, and run the finite-size decision at every size up to that bound. If a witness exists, the bound promises one in this finite range. If none is found, the instance is negative. This contradicts the externally conditional noncomputability theorem.','GeneralSemidecision:no_computable_witness_bound_degree',children=['finite-search','external-group'])
note('s6-4','From input bits to the semantic answer','Validation comes before canonicalisation and network construction. Canonicalisation merges syntactically equal nodes. Testing unequal identifiers detects impossible tests. The flow computation returns the semantic cut size, so comparing it with k implements the proved strict criterion.','Parsing:decideBits_iff_StrictBits FlowCut:rhoFlow_eq FlowCut:strictDecideP_iff MMain:program_accepts_iff',children=['canonical','split-network'])
note('s6-5','Why the running-time bound is about a machine','The paper counts scans, comparisons and augmentations. Claude’s extension goes further: it specifies primitive RAM transitions, compiles one fixed program, proves its phase contracts and combines their step bounds. The final theorem bounds both the number of transitions and their total logarithmic cost for every input, including malformed encodings.','MMain:mainM_spec MMain:program_run MMain:program_polynomial',children=['machine-model','machine-compilation','machine-cost','machine-phases'])
note('machine-model','What one machine step means','A configuration consists of a program counter and a memory function with natural-number cells. Primitive instructions copy constants or cells, load and store indirectly, add, subtract with truncation, and jump or halt. The step relation states one transition. Run composes finitely many such transitions and adds their costs.','Ram:Prim Ram:Instr Ram:DisequalityDispersion.Machine.step Ram:Run MMain:program MMain:initCfg')
note('machine-compilation','Why the structured program is the same RAM computation','The intermediate command language offers sequencing, branches and loops. Compilation replaces these constructs by primitive instructions and numerical jump destinations. The compiler correctness theorem preserves the final memory and the exact number of machine steps.','Machine:Cmd Machine:compile Machine:compile_correct MMain:program_run')
note('machine-cost','Why unit steps also give a polynomial bit cost','The allowed arithmetic can increase the maximum stored bit length by at most one per step, after accounting for constants. Instruction cost charges the bit lengths of accessed values and indirect addresses. A run of n steps therefore has cost bounded using its initial bit bound plus n. Composing this with the polynomial step bound yields a polynomial cost bound.','Ram:Run.bounded Ram:Run.cost_le MMain:program_constBound MMain:program_polynomial')
note('machine-phases','Every phase has a proved contract','The parse phase produces the encoded records or a failure flag. Validation checks references and arities. Canonicalisation merges syntax. Network construction builds the prescribed arcs. The augmenting-flow loop computes the cut value. Phase contracts state outputs, preserved memory and step bounds, and are combined in mainM_spec.','MParseRec:decodeInputM_spec MValid:gValidM_spec MCanon:canonIdsM_spec MNet:netM_spec MFlow:rhoM_spec MMain:mainM_spec')
note('s6-6','Why the undecidable degree is exactly the cut degree','When k<ρ, the routing lower bound eventually beats bₖ. When k>ρ, the sharp upper bound stays below bₖ at every size. Only k=ρ asks whether the exact sharp bound is ever reached. The eventual strict formulation follows from the same integer degree gap.','DegreeThreshold:not_lowerT_of_cut_lt GeneralAsymptotics:strictT_iff_eventual GeneralAsymptotics:eventual_h_iff')
note('s6-7','The three-output simplification','If the third term uses only x and y, the pair determines it, giving a two-term cut. A path from another variable to that term cannot pass through the variable vertices x or y. It supplies the third disjoint path. Routing then gives the cubic lower bound and its explicit comparison with n^(5/2).','Threshold:eval_congr_on_uses Asymptotics:c3_equivalences Asymptotics:five_halves_lower Asymptotics:theta_cubic',children=['zero-paths','menger'])
note('s6-8','Why the retained-output hypothesis cannot be removed','When x and y are not outputs, satisfying x≠y places no restriction on the independent output values z,w. At n≥2, choose a distinct x,y pair and then any z,w. This gives all n² outputs although the output cut is two.','GeneralExamples:outside_cutSize GeneralExamples:outside_dispersion GeneralExamples:outside_exceeds_threshold')

note('shift-step','Every signed letter is the same cyclic shift','Both positive and formal inverse symbols are assigned z↦z+1 modulo n. A written word of length L therefore evaluates as z↦z+L. If 0<L<n, its residue is nonzero, so no point is fixed. Nothing here asserts that the formal inverse symbols actually give inverses.','Core:evalWord_shift Core:shift_word_fixedPointFree Density:shiftTable Density:signedTables_shift')
note('s7-1','The approximation theorem and its limit','The shift construction gives a fixed-point-free word. Row counting loses at most M additional second coordinates per first coordinate. Dividing the resulting bounds by n(n−1) squeezes the density between 1−M/(n−1) and 1. The final convergence theorem is proved in Lean, not only stated as an asymptotic slogan.','Density:compiledMax_int_bounds Density:compiledMax_defect Density:compiledRatio_tendsto',children=['shift-step','density-limit'])
note('s7-2','Count the excluded second coordinates','At a fixed a, the guard forbids a and each of the M laws can forbid at most one further value. These values may coincide, which only improves the lower bound. Since the word has no fixed point, no entire row is removed. Multiply the row lower bound by n.','Core:card_forbiddenRow_le Core:code_card_lower Density:compiledMax_lower',children=['shift-step','integer-lower'])
note('s7-3','Formal inverse symbols are initially independent','The signature supplies two named functions; it imposes no equation relating them. Assigning both the same shift is a legal interpretation. The inverse equations become compulsory only when the image saturates the pair bound.','Semantics:Interpretation Core:signedTables Core:compiledPerfect_iff',children=['s4-5'])
note('s7-4','Why only distinct nonzero residues matter','A law word of written length ℓ forbids the second coordinate a+ℓ modulo n. Zero residue repeats the guard’s forbidden value a. Equal residues repeat each other. Thus each row has exactly n−1−|Rₙ| choices and the whole image has n times this cardinality.','Density:residues Density:forbiddenRow_shift_card Density:compiledPairs_shift_card_exact')
note('s7-5','Turning an error tolerance into a witness','Choose n larger than the written word length and large enough that M/(n−1)≤ε. The row bound then gives the required proportion. The formal definition epsAlphabet uses a natural-number ceiling. Since ε>0 and M≥0, the quantity being rounded is positive, so this agrees with the paper’s displayed ceiling formula. Open the definition and guarantee to inspect the casts and inequalities.','Density:epsAlphabet Density:epsAlphabet_gt Density:density_guarantee',children=['density-limit'])
note('density-limit','The density convergence, including its denominator','For large n, n≥2 and the denominator n(n−1) is positive. The deficit ratio is at most M/(n−1), which tends to zero with the presentation fixed. The squeeze argument then gives limit one. The hypothesis that the written word is nonempty is used to construct the fixed-point-free shift.','Density:compiledRatio_bounds Density:compiledRatio_tendsto')
note('s7-6','Approximate witnesses leave an exact decision unresolved','A bound on a fraction of missing pairs does not say whether that number can be zero. The reduction characterises zero deficit by exact group laws; the approximate constructions need not be group actions. There is therefore no decision procedure for saturation hidden in the computable approximation.','Density:density_guarantee CompilerBridge:hard_threshold_iff_quotient',children=['external-group','s7-7'])
note('s7-7','Written length differs from the represented group element','A nonempty word may represent the group identity. In every group action it then acts trivially, so it cannot separate in a quotient and saturation is impossible. But under the unrelated shift tables its value depends on written length. Free reduction preserves group evaluation, not arbitrary interpretation of the two signed symbols.','ReducedWords:groupWord_reduce CompilerBridge:hard_threshold_iff_quotient Core:evalWord_shift',children=['shift-step'])
note('s7-8','Padding preserves the density ratio exactly','Both the actual image and its sharp upper bound acquire the same positive factor n^(k−2). For n≥2 it cancels from the ratio. The approximation bound multiplies by this factor, and convergence follows from equality with the pair ratio.','Padding:padding_dispersion GeneralAsymptotics:paddedRatio_eq GeneralAsymptotics:paddedRatio_tendsto',children=['padding-step','density-limit'])

note('s8-1','Computed nodes are not additional independent sources','Flattening introduces names for determined values. Its unique-extension theorem does not turn those values into arbitrary source inputs. The undecidability reduction therefore remains a result about tests on computed terms, and does not settle the narrower source-only test language.','Flattening:defines_unique TupleFlattening:flatImageG_eq',children=['s2-5'],kind='scope')
note('s8-2','The problem without tests remains outside this result','This paragraph states the scope of the paper and the open problem. It is not a claim that Lean proves an undecidability result without tests. The cited earlier results and editorial statements remain in the bibliography; the formal dependency graph here certifies only the actual theorem statements it exports.',kind='scope')
note('s8-3','What the machine checks certify','Claude’s 71 modules are included unchanged. The proof explorer distinguishes exact declarations and proof terms from these authored explanations. The audit records are supplied separately. The group noncomputability input remains an explicit theorem hypothesis; standard foundational axioms appear by name at the bottom of the graph.','GeneralInterface:all_degree_headline MMain:program_polynomial',children=['external-group','verification-correspondence'])
note('s8-4','The precise machine theorem and its remaining boundary','The fixed RAM program is proved to halt with the answer decideBits for every input. The accepted language is StrictBits. The time polynomial is uniform in the encoded instance and the binary degree. A simulation of this logarithmic-cost RAM by a multitape Turing machine is not formalised in the project.','MMain:program_polynomial MMain:program_accepts_iff',children=['machine-model','machine-compilation','machine-cost'])
note('s8-5','How to audit the correspondence','Start from a paper paragraph, expand the mathematical reasoning, then open its exact Lean statements. Check the quantifiers and hypotheses before following the proof. Source steps expose recorded before/after goals. The kernel view exposes the proof expression, implicit arguments, dependencies and logical primitives. These exports aid inspection; the Lean kernel, not the web interface or its English explanations, checks the proof.','GeneralSemantics:dispersionTuple GeneralInterface:all_degree_headline MMain:program_polynomial',children=['verification-correspondence'])
note('verification-correspondence','Two different things to inspect','A successfully checked proof establishes its formal type. It does not establish that an English paragraph was translated faithfully. The correspondence notes explain identifications such as finite supremum versus maximum, pointwise identity versus equality of functions, and encoded validity versus membership of the input class. They are authored commentary and remain open to mathematical review.','GeneralSemantics:dispersionTuple_ge_iff Core:avoidance_iff_identity Parsing:decideBits_iff_StrictBits',kind='correspondence')
note('abstract','The paper’s main message','The two languages have the same input class and differ only by one unit in the output threshold. The strict side has a uniform polynomial machine algorithm. The non-strict side inherits undecidability from the stated external group theorem. The formal links below separate the semantic, algorithmic and external obligations.',children=['s1-6','external-group'])

nodes['forall-use']['sources']=['Core:avoidance_iff_identity']
(ROOT/'content/notes.json').write_text(json.dumps(nodes,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
print(len(nodes),'authored explanation nodes')
